# Is "rational choice" a theory or a tautology?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "If anything you do can be called rational, what does the theory forbid? What is revealed preference?"
next: {"title":"Who are you — and does it change what you choose?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/98-who-are-you-and-does-it-change-what-you.cast"}

## What would prove it wrong?
sources:
    - id: popper
      title: Logik der Forschung (The Logic of Scientific Discovery); Conjectures and Refutations
      authors: Karl Popper
      year: 1934
      finding: A theory is scientific only if it is falsifiable; Adler's psychology could explain both a man who pushes a child into water and one who saves it.
    - id: samuelson1938
      title: A Note on the Pure Theory of Consumer's Behaviour
      authors: Paul A. Samuelson
      year: 1938
      finding: 'Consumer theory restated in terms of observed choices: revealed preference.'

    box c1 "Votes" #2f6b8f width 290 height 78 radius 12 icon "ballot box" font_size 32 in board hidden true
    box r1 "taste for duty" #2f6b8f width 290 height 78 radius 12 font_size 32 in board hidden true
    box c2 "Stays home" #b5482e width 290 height 78 radius 12 icon house font_size 32 in board hidden true
    box r2 "weaker taste" #b5482e width 290 height 78 radius 12 font_size 32 in board hidden true
    box c3 "Gives a tenth" #2f6b8f width 290 height 78 radius 12 icon gift font_size 32 in board hidden true
    box r3 "enjoys giving" #2f6b8f width 290 height 78 radius 12 font_size 32 in board hidden true
    box c4 "Gives nothing" #b5482e width 290 height 78 radius 12 icon "money bag" font_size 32 in board hidden true
    box r4 "enjoys keeping" #b5482e width 290 height 78 radius 12 font_size 32 in board hidden true
    grid board gap 24 columns 2 fit.h 500 fit.w 650 fit.x 20 fit.y 150 hidden true
    label h1 "Choice" above #8f887c attach_to c1 font_size 30 hidden true
    label h2 "Why it's \"rational\"" above #8f887c attach_to r1 font_size 30 hidden true
    portrait popper x 840 y 510 width 180 of "Karl Popper" cameo true hidden true
    scratch adler work ["Adler explains:","pushes a child in ✓","dives in to save ✓"] x 840 y 480 font_size 28 hidden true
    text stamp "explains\neverything" #b5482e x 840 y 250 font_size 44 hidden true
    text q "What does it forbid?" #3d3833 x 500 y 420 font_size 56 hidden true
    text rp1 "Revealed preference" #2f6b8f x 500 y 525 font_size 52 hidden true
    text rp2 "could afford it + didn't choose it" #3d3833 x 500 y 290 font_size 36 hidden true
    text rp3 "= revealed less preferred" #3d3833 x 500 y 240 font_size 36 hidden true
    icon bk1 size 90 set twemoji x 380 y 410 icon_look picture of basket hidden true
    icon bk2 size 90 set twemoji x 620 y 410 icon_look picture of basket style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    text lbk1 "chosen ✓" #2f6b8f x 250 y 410 font_size 32 hidden true
    text lbk2 "affordable" #8f887c x 760 y 410 font_size 32 hidden true
    text sam "Samuelson, 1938" #8f887c cites ["samuelson1938"] x 500 y 185 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "What would prove it wrong?"

Is "rational choice" a theory, or a tautology? If anything you do can be called rational, what does the theory forbid?
    draw h1 h2

B: Let's try it. Someone votes, though her one vote will almost never decide anything. Ration (@draw r1@)al: she has a taste for doing her duty.
    draw c1

Her neighbour stays at home. Also ration (@draw r2@)al: his taste for duty is weaker.
    draw c2

B: One man gives a tenth of his pay to ch (@draw r3@)arity: he enjoys giving. Hi (@draw c4@)s brother giv (@draw r4@)es nothing: he enjoys keeping.
    draw c3

Every choice, and its exact opposite, gets a reason. That should worry us more than it reassures us.
    draw stamp

B: The philosopher Karl Popper put that worry sharply: a theory is scientific only if some observation could prove it wrong.
    draw popper

    erase popper

His warning case was Alfred Adler's psychology. A man who pu (@draw adler_line_2@)shes a child into the water, and a man (@draw adler_line_3@) who dives in to save one: Adler could explain both.
    draw adler_box adler_line_1

B: To Popper, that wasn't a strength. A theory that fits every possible event tells you nothing about which one will happen.
    highlight adler

    erase adler

So here's the test for rational choice:
    fade board stamp to 0.15

is there any pattern of choices it forbids?
    draw q

B: My bet is no. Show me any shopping, and I'll invent a taste that fits it.
    highlight q effect underline

    quiz "Knowing nothing about someone's tastes, could any pattern of choices still prove them inconsistent?" judge false voice b
        right: Hold that thought: there is one, and it needs no tastes at all.
        * No: some taste always fits
        * Yes: some patterns

    erase board stamp h1 h2

    move q by [0,200] scale 0.7 duration 0.8

Revealed preference, Samuelson's familiar idea, says yes: if y (@draw bk1 lbk1@)ou could afford b (@draw bk2 lbk2@)oth baskets and took this o (@draw rp2 rp3@)ne, you've shown which you prefer. That's en (@highlight rp1 effect underline@)ough to catch someone out.
    draw rp1 sam

    pause 0.3

## Two weeks, two price tags
