    x:
      - 0
      - 11
    'y':
      - 0
      - 11
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 150
      w: 610
      h: 520
vars:
    a:
      value: 4
      min: 0
      max: 5
    b:
      value: 2
      min: 0
      max: 10
    c1:
      expr: 80-12*a
    c2:
      expr: 20+6*b
    v:
      expr: max(0,min(1,(a-3.34)*6))*max(0,min(1,(3.32-b)*6))

    axes ax x_label Fish y_label Bread cross corner ticks.x [2,4,6,8,10] ticks.y [2,4,6,8,10] hidden true
    curve lineA #2f6b8f expr 10-2*x x_from 0 x_to 5 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    curve lineB #b5482e expr 5-0.5*x x_from 0 x_to 10 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text legA "Week 1: fish $8, bread $4" #2f6b8f x 815 y 625 font_size 30 hidden true
    text legB "Week 2: fish $4, bread $8" #b5482e x 815 y 580 font_size 30 hidden true
    text budget "Petter: $40 a week" #8f887c x 815 y 535 font_size 28 hidden true
    dot p1 #2f6b8f at.on lineA at.x 4 bind {"at.x":"a"} hidden true
    label l_p1 "Week 1" right #2f6b8f attach_to p1 font_size 26 hidden true
    dot p2 #b5482e at.on lineB at.x 2 bind {"at.x":"b"} hidden true
    label l_p2 "Week 2" above #b5482e attach_to p2 font_size 26 hidden true
    scratch calc work ["Basket 1, week-2 prices:","4×$4 + 2×$8 = $32","Basket 2, week-1 prices:","2×$8 + 4×$4 = $32"] x 815 y 390 font_size 27 hidden true
    arrow a12 p1 -> p2 curved #2f6b8f hidden true
    arrow a21 p2 -> p1 curved #b5482e hidden true
    text stamp "Forbidden:\nweak axiom" #b5482e x 815 y 400 font_size 44 hidden true
    text c1 "Basket 1, week-2 prices: ${c1:0}" #2f6b8f x 815 y 450 font_size 26 hidden true
    text c2 "Basket 2, week-1 prices: ${c2:0}" #b5482e x 815 y 405 font_size 26 hidden true
    text v_bad "Violation" #b5482e x 815 y 320 font_size 48 bind {"style.opacity":"v"} style.opacity 1 hidden true
    text v_ok "Consistent" #5e8c4f x 815 y 320 font_size 48 bind {"style.opacity":"1-v"} style.opacity 0 hidden true
    text garp "Many weeks → GARP:\nno loops" #3d3833 x 830 y 245 font_size 30 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mcard work [{"tex":"p_t,\\ x_t:\\ \\text{week } t"},{"tex":"p_1\\cdot x_2 \\le p_1\\cdot x_1 \\Rightarrow x_1 \\text{ beats } x_2"},{"tex":"\\text{WARP: not also } p_2\\cdot x_1 \\le p_2\\cdot x_2"},{"tex":"\\text{GARP: chains too}"}] x 800 y 380 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Two weeks, two price tags"

To catch someone out, you need two choices at two sets of prices. Take Petter, a deckhand in Harbou (@draw budget@)rtown, with forty dollars a week for fish and bread. The prices are illustrative.
    draw ax

B: Week one, fish is dear: eight dollars, and bread four. Every basket he can afford lies on this steep line: five fish, ten loaves, or a mix.
    draw lineA legA

    pause 0.3

Week two, the prices swap: fish four, bread eight. A flatter line, crossing the first.
    draw lineB legB

B: In week one, he buys four fish and two loaves.
    draw p1 l_p1

In week two, with fish at half the price, he buys two fish and four loaves.
    draw p2 l_p2

B: Odd, but fine. Maybe he just likes variety.
    highlight p1 p2

    quiz "Can one steady set of tastes explain both weeks?"
        right: No. Each basket was affordable in the week he chose the other.
        wrong: Check week two: could he still have bought week one's basket? He could, so here: no.
        * Yes
        + No

Take week two first. At week-two prices, his week-one basket costs just thirty-t (@highlight p1 lineB@)wo dollars. He could have had it, and chose the other.
    draw calc_box calc_line_1 calc_line_2

B: Now week one. At week-one prices, the week-two basket also costs thirty (@highlight p2 lineA@)-two. He could have had it, and chose the other.
    draw calc_line_3 calc_line_4

    erase calc

So each basket beats the other. No steady ranking can say that, and this is exactly what the theory forbids: t (@draw stamp@)he weak axiom of revealed preference.
    draw a12 a21

    pause 0.5

B: Put plainly: he bought more fish when fish was dear a (@point at.ref p2 gesture circle@)nd less when it was cheap, though either basket was within reach in either week.
    point at.ref p1 gesture circle

    erase a12 a21 stamp

    draw c1 c2 v_bad v_ok

Your turn: slide each week's basket along its line and watch the verdict. Only one corner of choices breaks the rule.
    explore params ["a","b"]

    animate a 4 b 2 duration 1

B: A pair passes whenever one basket was out of reach in the other week.
    animate a 2 duration 2.5

B: With many weeks, the test becomes a chain, called GARP: no loop of 'this beats that' may come back round.
    draw garp

    pause 0.3

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own store m default "Skip it"

@math

    erase mathq

    hide c1 c2 v_bad v_ok garp

    animate a 4 duration 1

In symbols: call week t's prices p-t and its basket x-t. Then p times x is what a basket costs at those prices.
    draw mcard_box mcard_line_1

If p-one times x-two is at most p-one times x-one, basket two was affordable in week one, so basket one is revealed preferred.
    draw mcard_line_2

The weak axiom: then p-two times x-one must not also be at most p-two times x-two. For Petter both hold: thirty-two dollars against forty, twice.
    draw mcard_line_3

And GARP: if any chain of choices says A beats B, then B must never have been chosen when A was strictly cheaper.
    draw mcard_line_4

    erase mcard

@test

    erase mathq

    show c1 c2 v_bad v_ok garp

    pause 0.3

## Do people pass — and is that enough?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 7-year-olds
      - 11-year-olds
      - Students
      - Random
    values:
      - 26
      - 62
      - 65
      - 2
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    colors:
      - null
      - null
      - null
      - grey
    y_label: No GARP violations (%)
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 80
    label_size: 26
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 170
      w: 560
      h: 470
sources:
    - id: afriat1967
      title: The Construction of Utility Functions from Expenditure Data
      authors: Sydney N. Afriat
      year: 1967
      finding: A finite set of choices can be explained by a well-behaved utility function if and only if it satisfies a cyclical consistency condition (GARP).
    - id: varian1982
      title: The Nonparametric Approach to Demand Analysis
      authors: Hal R. Varian
      year: 1982
      finding: GARP as a practical test of consumer data.
    - id: harbaugh2001
      title: 'GARP for Kids: On the Development of Rational Choice Behavior'
      authors: William T. Harbaugh, Kate Krause, Timothy R. Berry
      year: 2001
      finding: '31 second graders, 42 sixth graders and 55 undergraduates chose from 11 budget sets of chips and juice; share with zero GARP violations: 26%, 62%, 65%; bootstrapped random choice: 2% (Table 2).'
      url: https://harbaugh.uoregon.edu/Papers/GARPforKids.pdf
    - id: choi2007
      title: Consistency and Heterogeneity of Individual Behavior under Uncertainty
      authors: Syngjoo Choi, Raymond Fisman, Douglas Gale, Shachar Kariv
      year: 2007
      finding: Berkeley subjects each chose from 50 randomly drawn budget lines; most came close to satisfying GARP, while all 25,000 simulated random subjects violated it.
    - id: choi2014
      title: Who Is (More) Rational?
      authors: Syngjoo Choi, Shachar Kariv, Wieland Müller, Dan Silverman
      year: 2014
      finding: In a large Dutch household panel (CentERpanel), consistency with utility maximization varies widely and is correlated with wealth.
    - id: sen1977
      title: 'Rational Fools: A Critique of the Behavioral Foundations of Economic Theory'
      authors: Amartya Sen
      year: 1977
      finding: A single all-purpose preference ordering cannot separate sympathy from commitment.
    - id: stiglerbecker1977
      title: De Gustibus Non Est Disputandum
      authors: George J. Stigler, Gary S. Becker
      year: 1977
      finding: Treat tastes as stable over time and similar among people; explain behaviour by prices and incomes.

    text afr "Afriat 1967:\npass GARP ⇔\nsteady tastes fit" #3d3833 cites ["afriat1967","varian1982"] x 820 y 560 font_size 34 hidden true
    text setup "Harbaugh et al. 2001\ncrisps & juice,\n11 choices" #8f887c cites ["harbaugh2001"] x 820 y 380 font_size 30 hidden true
    text choi "Adults, 2007:\nmost close\nto passing" #2f6b8f cites ["choi2007"] x 820 y 560 font_size 34 hidden true
    text dutch "Dutch panel:\nmore consistent,\nricher" #2f6b8f cites ["choi2014"] x 820 y 380 font_size 32 hidden true
    text hA "Consistency alone" #3d3833 x 255 y 600 font_size 44 hidden true
    text hB "+ stable tastes" #3d3833 x 745 y 600 font_size 44 hidden true
    text a1 "Sen: a \"rational fool\"" #b5482e cites ["sen1977"] x 255 y 505 font_size 38 hidden true
    text a2 "tastes shift?\n→ forbids nothing" #b5482e x 255 y 400 font_size 38 hidden true
    text b0 "Stigler & Becker, 1977" #8f887c cites ["stiglerbecker1977"] x 745 y 505 font_size 34 hidden true
    text b1 "predicts: dearer\n→ buy less" #5e8c4f x 745 y 420 font_size 38 hidden true
    text b2 "…and can be refuted" #b5482e x 745 y 335 font_size 38 hidden true
    text s1 "Testable:\nconsensus" #5e8c4f x 255 y 225 font_size 44 hidden true
    text s2 "Enough alone:\ncontested" #b5482e x 745 y 225 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Do people pass — and is that enough?"

So the theory can fail. Sydney Afriat proved in 1967 that passing GARP is exactly what it takes for some steady tastes to explain every choice, and Hal Varian made it a routine test in 1982.
    draw afr

B: So, does anyone pass? In 2001 William Harbaugh and two colleagues tested children, with eleven choices between bundles of crisps and juice boxes.
    draw axes setup

Among seven-year-olds, about a quarter made no violations at all.
    draw bar_1

    ask "Guess: what share of 11-year-olds made no violations at all? Drag the bar." on bar_2 store g default "30"
        right: Sixty-two percent: close to your {g}.
        wrong: You said {g}: it was sixty-two percent.

B: Eleven-year-olds: sixty-two percent. University students: sixty-five, barely better.
    draw bar_3

And choosing at random? Simulated choosers passed just two percent of the time. The test has teeth, and most people clear it.
    draw bar_4

    pause 0.5

    erase afr setup

B: Adults on screens look similar. In a 2007 Berkeley study, most students came close to passing on fifty budget lines each, and every simulated random chooser failed.
    draw choi

And in a large Dutch panel, the more consistent choosers were also the wealthier.
    draw dutch

    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 choi dutch

B: But passing is a low bar. Amartya Sen, in 1977, called the merely consistent chooser a rational fool: one ranking for everything, with no room for commitment.
    draw hA a1

And there's a deeper trap. If Petter's tastes may change between weeks, he's off the hook, and the theory forbids nothing again.
    draw a2

B: Which is why Stigler and Becker's rule, also from 1977, matters here: treat tastes as stable and much alike.
    draw hB b0

That's the strength: add stable, mostly self-interested tastes, and the theory predicts, for instance, that people buy less of what gets dearer, Giffen oddities aside.
    draw b1

B: And the weakness: every assumption added to give it content is one that can fail, and tastes do change.
    draw b2

How sure are we? That rational choice forbids something testable: consensus. That consis (@draw s2@)tency alone makes a useful theory of behaviour: contested.
    draw s1

    pause 0.5

    erase s1 s2

    quiz "After a pay rise, a shopper buys a basket she couldn't afford before. Does that break the weak axiom?"
        right: No: the new basket was out of reach before, so choosing it now contradicts nothing.
        wrong: Ask whether each basket was affordable when the other was chosen: here, no, the new one was out of reach before.
        * Yes: she switched baskets
        + No: the new basket was out of reach before
        * Only if prices changed too

    quiz "A gym member switches plans every month and says her tastes changed each time. If every such excuse is allowed, what does the theory forbid?" voice b
        right: Nothing at all. The content comes from holding tastes still.
        wrong: Any switch can be blamed on a change of taste: here, nothing at all.
        + Nothing at all
        * Only switching when prices fall
        * Only random choices

    quiz "Someone keeps a costly promise and passes every GARP test. What does the test tell you about why?"
        right: Nothing about why. It checks that choices hang together, not what drives them.
        wrong: GARP looks only at prices and baskets, never at reasons: here, nothing about why.
        + Nothing: it checks consistency, not motives
        * That he is secretly selfish
        * That he is irrational

So: a theory, but (@draw s1 s2@) a thin one. On its own it forbids only contradictions you could spot on two receipts. What it says about the world comes from what we assume people want.

    pause 0.5

## Where next
