    '|a||No. Each basket was affordable in the week he chose the other.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3760
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||Each basket was affordable in the week he chose the other.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3800
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||Take week two first. At week-two prices, his week-one basket costs just thirty-two dollars. He could have had it, and chose the other.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8680
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Now week one. At week-one prices, the week-two basket also costs thirty-two. He could have had it, and chose the other.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6840
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:6yo702'
    '|a||So each basket beats the other. No steady ranking can say that, and this is exactly what the theory forbids: the weak axiom of revealed preference.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10600
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:135yob2'
    '|b||Put plainly: he bought more fish when fish was dear and less when it was cheap, though either basket was within reach in either week.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8560
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:jayv4j'
    '|b||A pair passes whenever one basket was out of reach in the other week.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4880
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|b||With many weeks, the test becomes a chain, called GARP: no loop of ''this beats that'' may come back round.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7600
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:10eangx'
    '|a||Show the math, or skip it?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2400
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:3elsui'
    '|a||In symbols: call week t''s prices p-t and its basket x-t. Then p times x is what a basket costs at those prices.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8960
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||If p-one times x-two is at most p-one times x-one, basket two was affordable in week one, so basket one is revealed preferred.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10160
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:w0riyt'
    '|a||The weak axiom: then p-two times x-one must not also be at most p-two times x-two. For Petter both hold: thirty-two dollars against forty, twice.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12120
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1jforee'
    '|a||And GARP: if any chain of choices says A beats B, then B must never have been chosen when A was strictly cheaper.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7600
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:x075ac'
    '|a||So the theory can fail. Sydney Afriat proved in 1967 that passing GARP is exactly what it takes for some steady tastes to explain every choice, and Hal Varian made it a routine test in 1982.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14200
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||So, does anyone pass? In 2001 William Harbaugh and two colleagues tested children, with eleven choices between bundles of crisps and juice boxes.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11040
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:12rf2pu'
    '|a||Among seven-year-olds, about a quarter made no violations at all.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4080
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:16loxyi'
    '|a||Guess: what share of 11-year-olds made no violations at all? Drag the bar.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1p4evu2'
    '|b||Eleven-year-olds: sixty-two percent. University students: sixty-five, barely better.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5600
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||And choosing at random? Simulated choosers passed just two percent of the time. The test has teeth, and most people clear it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8360
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:h2p0ts'
    '|b||Adults on screens look similar. In a 2007 Berkeley study, most students came close to passing on fifty budget lines each, and every simulated random chooser failed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9920
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:12nx0tu'
    '|a||And in a large Dutch panel, the more consistent choosers were also the wealthier.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5400
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1ihx0ve'
    '|b||But passing is a low bar. Amartya Sen, in 1977, called the merely consistent chooser a rational fool: one ranking for everything, with no room for commitment.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12200
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1uot202'
    '|a||And there''s a deeper trap. If Petter''s tastes may change between weeks, he''s off the hook, and the theory forbids nothing again.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7960
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1qi7vv2'
    '|b||Which is why Stigler and Becker''s rule, also from 1977, matters here: treat tastes as stable and much alike.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7280
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:iznlus'
    '|a||That''s the strength: add stable, mostly self-interested tastes, and the theory predicts, for instance, that people buy less of what gets dearer, Giffen oddities aside.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11520
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:zztqzy'
    '|b||And the weakness: every assumption added to give it content is one that can fail, and tastes do change.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:87ebn8'
    '|a||How sure are we? That rational choice forbids something testable: consensus. That consistency alone makes a useful theory of behaviour: contested.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9600
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1hi50vc'
    '|a||After a pay rise, a shopper buys a basket she couldn''t afford before. Does that break the weak axiom?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6600
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:esp5po'
    '|a||No: the new basket was out of reach before, so choosing it now contradicts nothing.':
